      "math_academy_id": "e-184",
      "problem": "Given that $y =\\dfrac{e^{3x}}{2^{x}+1},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=e^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=2^x +1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nu'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad v'(x) = 2^x \\ln 2.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t&= \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t        \t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x} \\cdot  \\left( 2^{x}+1\\right) - e^{3x} \\cdot \\left(2^x \\cdot \\ln2\\right)}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{3e^{3x}2^{x} + 3e^{3x} - e^{3x}2^{x}\\ln 2}{\\left(2^{ x}+1 \\right)^2}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{e^{3x}  \\left( 3(2^{x}) - 2^{x} \\ln 2 +3\\right)}{\\left(2^{x}+1 \\right)^2}\n\t\t}\n$$",
